    <bioghist>
      <head>Historical Note</head>
      <p>Welsh immigrants arrived in Centerville, Allegany County, New York, around 1840. They
        largely settled in the southwest part of the town; the hamlet would later be called
        Fairview. The Welsh Congregational Church was gathered in 1846. The congregants initially
        met at a schoolhouse in Freedom just across the town and county border. In 1847, the first
        church building was constructed on the southern corner of Findlay Road and County Line Road
        just across the street from the Freedom schoolhouse. New church buildings were constructed
        closer to the Fairview hamlet in 1865 and again in 1902. The church dissolved around 1930;
        different sources suggest 1928 and 1936 as possible dates of dissolution.</p>
    </bioghist>
    <scopecontent>
      <head>Scope and Contents</head>
      <p>This collection contains administrative and membership records for the Fairview Welsh
        Congregational Church in Centerville, Allegany County, New York. The collection includes
        financial records, meeting minutes, membership records, articles of agreement for property
        between the church and the towns of Freedom and Centerville, and correspondence with the
        Home Missionary Society.</p>
    </scopecontent>
    <arrangement>
      <head>Arrangement</head>
      <p>Materials in this collection have been arranged into chronological order by start date.</p>
    </arrangement>
